{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"content":null,"reasoning":"We need to answer three questions, each with verdict, justification (2-3 sentences), strongest counter-evidence (Riss), and if uncertain, explicitly \"pending\" + what measurement missing.\n\nWe must not invent facts; only use given evidence. So we must base answers solely on provided text.\n\nLet's parse each question.\n\nQuestion 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Where is receipt recorded in token ledger? Options A-D. Also subquestion about `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks, S2 continue list but with state column.\n\nWe need to decide based on evidence.\n\nEvidence: \"Heute lebt der Zustand eines Tokens (xfer) nur im Ledger des Absenders (Emitter). Der Empfänger kann seinen eigenen Eingang nicht quittieren: `xfer close` auf einen fremden Token ist ein harter Fehler. Quittung und Selbst-Erledigung sind beide `CLOSED` — nicht unterscheidbar. Zusätzlich listet `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` geschlossene Blöcke weiter; `--fired` bleibt 0, Erledigtes liest sich als offen. Ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde zurückgenommen (Commit eb2f1b749, −202 Zeilen).\"\n\nThus currently receipt is not recorded; they tried an ack arm but removed it. Options: A: own act ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz> in receiver's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED. B: as today: receiver writes CLOSED into emitter's ledger. C: no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger. D: a ledger per token instead of per line (both write same storage).\n\nGiven evidence: currently receipt not possible; they removed ack arm. So they are not doing A. B says receiver writes CLOSED into emitter's ledger; but evidence says receiver cannot close foreign token; it's a hard error. So B is not correct. C says receipt state derived read-only from token ledger; but currently they cannot distinguish receipt vs self-completion; both are CLOSED. So deriving receipt state read-only would not solve distinguishability. D suggests per-token ledger; not mentioned.\n\nWe need to pick best based on evidence: The evidence indicates they tried an ack arm and removed it; currently they have no receipt; they cannot close foreign token; they list closed blocks but still show as open. So maybe they need to implement A? But we must not invent facts; we can only say based on evidence what is true. The question: \"Wo wird die Quittung im Token-Ledger belegt?\" Options. We need to give verdict based on evidence. Since evidence shows they removed ack arm, and currently no receipt is recorded; they cannot close foreign token; they list closed blocks but still as open. So the current state is that receipt is not recorded; maybe option C (no new act: receipt state derived read-only) is closest to current behavior? But they said receipt and self-completion both CLOSED not distinguishable. That matches \"no new act: receipt state derived read-only\" because they'd just read the token ledger state (which is CLOSED for both). However they also said they list closed blocks further; `--fired` stays 0, completed reads as open. That seems like they are trying to derive receipt but failing.\n\nThus the strongest counter-evidence: The earlier ack arm was removed, indicating they do not want A. Also receiver cannot close foreign token, contradicting B. So maybe C is best.\n\nThus verdict: C.\n\nNow subquestion `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks vs S2 continue list but with state column. Evidence: \"Zusätzlich listet `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` geschlossene Blöcke weiter; `--fired` bleibt 0, Erledigtes liest sich als offen.\" So currently it lists closed blocks further (i.e., does not hide them). So S2 (continue list, but with state column) matches evidence: they list closed blocks, but they lack state column? Actually they list closed blocks further; `--fired` stays 0, completed reads as open. So they are listing closed blocks but not distinguishing. So S2 is closer: continue list, but with state column (they lack state column). So answer: S2.\n\nThus for Q1: Verdikt: C; Begründung: ...; Riss: ...; plus subquestion: S2.\n\nNow Q2: R3 from R-2026-10-17-a: Concurrency key of register-coverage. Should we change to group: register-coverage-${{ github.sha }} with cancel-in-progress: true (like ci-gate since 2026-10-08) instead of current group: …-${{ github.ref }} with false?\n\nEvidence: \"Gemessen: ein Push auf main feuert 5–7 Gates; letzte 100 Läufe = 25 verschiedene HEAD-SHAs; in 10 Minuten acht `register-coverage`-Läufe, sieben `cancelled` — in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, `cancel-in-progress: false` schützt nur den laufenden. Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, kein je-SHA-Artefakt.\"\n\nAlso: Vorrunde: 3:3 Patt. A per-SHA (GLM, DeepSeek, Lumo: „der HEAD ist je SHA definiert\") · B per-ref (Fugu, Mistral, Kimi: „die Adresse des HEAD ist der Ref, per-SHA adressiert die Vergangenheit\"). Kimi praktisch: ein Cancel mid-write kann inkonsistenten Zustand hinterlassen; das Gate muss `cancelled` ignorieren.\n\nOptions: A per-SHA + true · B per-ref + true (nur der jüngste Lauf lebt) · C Debounce/Batch.\n\nWe need to decide based on evidence. Evidence shows many cancellations; current setup (per-ref + false) leads to many cancelled runs because new run cancels waiting predecessors? Actually \"in einer Concurrency-Gruppe löscht ein neuer Lauf die wartenden Vorgänger, cancel-in-progress: false schützt nur den laufenden.\" Means with false, new run cancels waiting predecessors (i.e., they get cancelled). That's what we see: many cancelled runs. The gate checks HEAD state of register, no per-SHA artifact. So using per-SHA would differentiate runs by SHA, reducing cancellations because each SHA gets its own group? Actually if group includes SHA, each run would have unique group, so no cancellation between different SHAs; but if same SHA (e.g., re-run) could cancel. But they have many different SHAs (25 distinct in last 100 runs). So per-SHA would likely reduce cancellations. However the gate only checks HEAD state, not per-SHA artifact, meaning that using SHA may not be necessary for correctness; but per-ref groups all runs on same ref (e.g., main) together, causing cancellations. The evidence suggests they want to reduce wasted cancellations; they consider per-SHA + true (option A) or per-ref + true (option B) or debounce/batch (C). Option B per-ref + true would keep only the newest run alive (cancel-in-progress: true cancels in-progress runs? Actually cancel-in-progress: true means if a new run starts, it cancels any currently running workflow? Need to recall GitHub Actions: cancel-in-progress: true cancels any currently running workflows of the same group when a new workflow is queued. So with per-ref + true, only the newest run lives, others cancelled. That would still cause cancellations but maybe less? Actually with false, new run cancels waiting predecessors (queued). With true, new run cancels any currently running workflows. The evidence shows many cancelled runs (likely queued). So switching to true might change which are cancelled but still many.\n\nOption A per-SHA + true: each SHA gets its own group, so runs on same SHA would cancel each other's in-progress; but different SHAs would not interfere. Since they have many SHAs, this would reduce cancellations significantly. However the gate only checks HEAD state, not per-SHA artifact, meaning that using SHA may be unnecessary but still safe? The evidence does not say it's unsafe. Kimi noted that a cancel mid-write could leave inconsistent state; gate must ignore cancelled. That's a concern regardless of grouping.\n\nThus the strongest evidence: many cancellations, gate checks HEAD state, no per-SHA artifact. The Vorrunde was tied 3:3. Kimi's practical note about cancel mid-write suggests need to handle cancelled. The question: Should we change to per-SHA + true? The evidence does not clearly support either; it's ambiguous. So we may need to answer pending, specifying what measurement missing: e.g., need to measure impact of per-SHA grouping on cancellation rate and correctness, or need to measure frequency of mid-write cancellations leading to inconsistency.\n\nThus verdict: pending; need to measure effect of per-SHA grouping on cancellation and correctness, and need to measure rate of mid-write cancellations causing inconsistency.\n\nNow Q3: R-2026-10-18-e Folge: Das Register kennt state/stimmen nicht. Evidence: register_lookup searches docs/{handover,surveys,plans,auftrag,blatt,concepts,paper} — but NOT state/stimmen/, where all council verdicts lie (measured 2026-10-18: grep -c stimmen = 0). Folge: a line asks \"what is open for me?\" and sees foreign verdicts only if council distributes them manually as letters. Lines may not change architecture themselves (R-2026-10-18-d/e: architecture paths need a RAT-ID). Verdikt-Format: ## An <linie> blocks at end of verdict file.\n\nOptions: A — add state/stimmen/ as additional doc root in reader. B — derive: verdicts migrate as ## An <linie> blocks into living handover of receiver line. C — new register-doc state/stimmen/_register.md (only index) as own source. D — status quo: council continues to distribute","reasoning_content":"We need to answer three questions, each with verdict, justification (2-3 sentences), strongest counter-evidence (Riss), and if uncertain, explicitly \"pending\" + what measurement missing.\n\nWe must not invent facts; only use given evidence. So we must base answers solely on provided text.\n\nLet's parse each question.\n\nQuestion 1: R-2026-10-18-a: Where is receipt recorded in token ledger? Options A-D. Also subquestion about `--addressed`: S1 hide closed blocks, S2 continue list but with state column.\n\nWe need to decide based on evidence.\n\nEvidence: \"Heute lebt der Zustand eines Tokens (xfer) nur im Ledger des Absenders (Emitter). Der Empfänger kann seinen eigenen Eingang nicht quittieren: `xfer close` auf einen fremden Token ist ein harter Fehler. Quittung und Selbst-Erledigung sind beide `CLOSED` — nicht unterscheidbar. Zusätzlich listet `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` geschlossene Blöcke weiter; `--fired` bleibt 0, Erledigtes liest sich als offen. Ein früher gebauter `xfer ack`-Arm wurde zurückgenommen (Commit eb2f1b749, −202 Zeilen).\"\n\nThus currently receipt is not recorded; they tried an ack arm but removed it. Options: A: own act ACK <tok> by=<linie>.<sitz> in receiver's ledger; ACKED distinguishable from CLOSED. B: as today: receiver writes CLOSED into emitter's ledger. C: no new act: receipt state derived read-only from token ledger. D: a ledger per token instead of per line (both write same storage).\n\nGiven evidence: currently receipt not possible; they removed ack arm. So they are not doing A. B says receiver writes CLOSED into emitter's ledger; but evidence says receiver cannot close foreign token; it's a hard error. So B is not correct. C says receipt state derived read-only from token ledger; but currently they cannot distinguish receipt vs self-completion; both are CLOSED. So deriving receipt state read-only would not solve distinguishability. D suggests per-token ledger; not mentioned.\n\nWe need to pick best based on evidence: The evidence indicates they tried an ack arm and removed it; currently they have no receipt; they cannot close foreign token; they list closed blocks but still show as open. So maybe they need to implement A? But we must not invent facts; we can only say based on evidence what is true. The question: \"Wo wird die Quittung im Token-Ledger belegt?\" Options. We need to give verdict based on evidence. Since evidence shows they removed ack arm, and currently no receipt is recorded; they cannot close foreign token; they list closed blocks but still as open. So the current state is that receipt is not recorded; maybe option C (no new act: receipt state derived read-only) is closest to current behavior? But they said receipt